WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.125
David: Welcome to LitShare.

2
00:00:02.825 --> 00:00:05.475
This is Protein Degradation English
Corner.

3
00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:06.788
Episode one.

4
00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:09.550
Nature, Christina M. Woo.

5
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.775
C R B N's Hidden Pocket: A Volume Knob for
Protein Degradation.

6
00:00:16.775 --> 00:00:18.025
Here is the question.

7
00:00:19.125 --> 00:00:23.150
Every drug that works through cereblon
binds to the same pocket.

8
00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:29.500
Is there a second pocket on this protein,
one that could tune those drugs up or
down?

9
00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:36.650
Alex: That is the question a team from
Harvard, Scripps, and G S K set out to
answer.

10
00:00:37.175 --> 00:00:38.425
Their paper is in Nature, twenty twenty-
six.

11
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:47.500
First author Vanessa Dippon, and the
corresponding authors are Christina Woo
and Andrew Benowitz.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.825
David: Before we get to the answer, what
does cereblon actually do?

13
00:00:53.850 --> 00:01:01.300
Alex: Cereblon, or C R B N, is a substrate
adapter for an E three ubiquitin ligase.

14
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.300
In plain words, it is a matchmaker.

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.600
Drugs like thalidomide and lenalidomide
bind to cereblon at a site called the
thalidomide binding domain.

16
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:21.050
Then cereblon grabs a new protein, a
neosubstrate, and tags it for destruction
by the proteasome.

17
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:25.850
David: And that is how lenalidomide treats
multiple myeloma.

18
00:01:26.525 --> 00:01:26.875
Alex: Exactly.

19
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:36.225
It makes cereblon degrade the Ikaros
proteins, I K Z F one and I K Z F three,
and also C K one alpha.

20
00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:42.100
That is why it works in myeloma and in del
five q M D S, a blood disorder.

21
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:49.600
But the same mechanism also degrades a
protein called S A L L four, which is
linked to birth defects.

22
00:01:50.375 --> 00:01:54.950
So the field has spent years designing new
molecules for that one binding site.

23
00:01:55.625 --> 00:01:56.650
Nobody had found a second one.

24
00:01:57.225 --> 00:01:59.725
David: So how did they find it?

25
00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.350
Alex: Almost by accident.

26
00:02:01.825 --> 00:02:07.400
The G S K team ran a high-throughput
screen to find new molecules for the known
site.

27
00:02:07.925 --> 00:02:14.050
They started with a ten thousand compound
validation set, using a light-based assay.

28
00:02:14.875 --> 00:02:18.225
Normally, when a molecule binds, the
signal goes down.

29
00:02:19.025 --> 00:02:21.450
But about fifty compounds did the
opposite.

30
00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.275
The signal went up.

31
00:02:23.275 --> 00:02:28.200
David: So instead of blocking the probe,
something was making it bind better.

32
00:02:28.625 --> 00:02:29.675
Alex: That was the clue.

33
00:02:30.375 --> 00:02:37.525
They ruled out artifacts, tested the
compounds one by one with mass
spectrometry, and a single molecule
survived.

34
00:02:38.325 --> 00:02:39.150
Its name is S B four zero five four eight
three.

35
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.950
David: How did they prove it binds
somewhere else, and not the same site?

36
00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:48.775
Alex: Two pieces of evidence.

37
00:02:49.375 --> 00:02:59.025
First, mass spectrometry showed that S B
four zero five four eight three and
pomalidomide can sit on cereblon at the
same time.

38
00:02:59.025 --> 00:03:02.575
If they shared one site, that would be
impossible.

39
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.775
Second, the numbers.

40
00:03:05.775 --> 00:03:11.250
The allosteric constant, gamma, came out
at ten, which means strong synergy.

41
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:23.625
The lenalidomide probe bound ten times
more tightly with the new molecule
present, and the new molecule also bound
ten times more tightly with the probe
present.

42
00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.225
David: Ten times in both directions.

43
00:03:27.075 --> 00:03:29.200
That is real cooperativity.

44
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.525
But where exactly is this second site?

45
00:03:32.525 --> 00:03:42.175
Alex: They solved the crystal structure at
two point three nine angstrom resolution,
with both lenalidomide and the new
molecule bound.

46
00:03:42.975 --> 00:03:56.075
The second pocket sits at the interface
between two domains of cereblon, the
helical bundle domain and the thalidomide
binding domain, about twenty-two angstroms
away from the known site.

47
00:03:56.675 --> 00:03:59.875
The molecule is anchored by two amino
acids, D two six five and R two eight
three.

48
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.625
David: The paper calls the pocket cryptic.

49
00:04:07.025 --> 00:04:08.500
What does that mean here?

50
00:04:09.275 --> 00:04:12.500
Alex: It means you cannot see it in the
known structures.

51
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.850
The pocket only opens when the right
molecule comes along.

52
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:25.375
A loop near the pocket is flexible, and
one amino acid, W two six four, has to
move out of the way.

53
00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.275
And the pocket looks important in nature.

54
00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:36.100
Two thirds of the amino acids that form it
are identical across the top five hundred
related sequences.

55
00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:41.575
David: Does the molecule actually work
inside cells, or only in a test tube?

56
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.325
Alex: It works in cells.

57
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.300
Three lines of evidence.

58
00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.975
First, it stabilizes cereblon in myeloma
cells, in a thermal shift assay.

59
00:04:52.250 --> 00:05:02.675
Second, in a live-cell binding assay, it
doubled the signal, and mutating D two six
five or R two eight three wiped out the
effect.

60
00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.000
That pins the effect to the new pocket.

61
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:11.275
Third, it boosted the degradation of C K
one alpha.

62
00:05:12.125 --> 00:05:25.900
With a lenalidomide analogue called D E G
four seven, the concentration needed for
half-maximal degradation dropped from ten
nanomolar to zero point seven five
nanomolar.

63
00:05:26.275 --> 00:05:28.975
David: That is about a thirteen-fold
shift.

64
00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.100
Did they find anything new with this tool?

65
00:05:32.825 --> 00:05:33.125
Alex: Yes.

66
00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:36.600
They used it as a filter in proteomics
experiments.

67
00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:47.075
Comparing cells treated with D E G four
seven, with and without the new molecule,
narrowed hundreds of changing proteins
down to a short list of six.

68
00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:53.500
One of them was a kinase called Wee one, a
new cereblon neosubstrate.

69
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:03.650
It has the G loop motif that cereblon
substrates share, and mutating one
glycine, G three two two, blocked its
degradation.

70
00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:06.325
David: The headline result is the survey.

71
00:06:07.075 --> 00:06:09.950
Eighty-nine ligands, six neosubstrates.

72
00:06:10.675 --> 00:06:11.700
What did they find?

73
00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.325
Alex: A clear split.

74
00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:23.025
For C K one alpha, Wee one, and G S P T
one, the new molecule boosted degradation
in nearly every case.

75
00:06:23.725 --> 00:06:31.600
One hundred percent for C K one alpha,
ninety-six for Wee one, eighty-two for G S
P T one.

76
00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:44.300
But for the zinc finger proteins, I K Z F
one and three, I K Z F two, and S A L L
four, it mostly did the opposite.

77
00:06:45.075 --> 00:06:50.600
Seventy-six percent of ligands showed
reduced I K Z F two degradation.

78
00:06:51.625 --> 00:06:55.200
In several cases it blocked degradation
completely.

79
00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:59.125
David: Was there any ligand that did not
respond at all?

80
00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:00.825
Alex: One exception.

81
00:07:02.575 --> 00:07:08.550
Mezigdomide, a clinical-stage drug that
grips cereblon at two domains at once,
showed no synergy at all.

82
00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:13.075
David: The same molecule boosts some
proteins and blocks others.

83
00:07:13.775 --> 00:07:14.200
How?

84
00:07:16.375 --> 00:07:17.925
Alex: Cryo electron microscopy gave the
answer.

85
00:07:18.725 --> 00:07:25.675
Cereblon switches between an open shape
and a closed shape, and the closed shape
is the one that grabs neosubstrates.

86
00:07:26.425 --> 00:07:32.975
The new molecule pushes cereblon toward
the closed shape, through a new
intermediate the authors call C R B N int.

87
00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:38.500
It doubled the share of cereblon in the
closed shape.

88
00:07:39.075 --> 00:07:43.225
But it also nudges the position of the
drug inside its own pocket.

89
00:07:43.225 --> 00:07:49.650
C K one alpha fits even better than
before, so its degradation goes up.

90
00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.050
I K Z F one fits worse, so its degradation
goes down.

91
00:07:55.575 --> 00:08:01.200
David: Does it also work with P R O T A
Cs, the two-headed degraders?

92
00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:02.075
Alex: Yes.

93
00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:16.225
It boosted degradation by several P R O T
A Cs, including B R D four degraders, a C
R B N homo P R O T A C, and F K B P twelve
degraders.

94
00:08:16.225 --> 00:08:27.175
In a proteomics experiment with a broad
kinase degrader, it revealed fourteen
strongly downregulated proteins, nine of
them kinases.

95
00:08:27.675 --> 00:08:28.800
David: What are the limitations?

96
00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:30.550
What is still missing?

97
00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:31.875
Alex: Three things.

98
00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:39.025
First, S B four zero five four eight three
is a tool compound, not a drug.

99
00:08:39.875 --> 00:08:43.825
It binds weakly on its own, about twenty-
five micromolar.

100
00:08:44.675 --> 00:08:48.375
Second, everything here is in cells and in
test tubes.

101
00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.625
There is no animal data yet.

102
00:08:50.625 --> 00:08:55.575
Third, we still do not know whether the
pocket has a natural job.

103
00:08:56.325 --> 00:09:05.575
It is conserved across species, and it
works in mouse cells too, which hints at
an endogenous role, but no natural ligand
is known.

104
00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:07.000
David: So what is the takeaway?

105
00:09:08.475 --> 00:09:10.450
Alex: Cereblon has a hidden second pocket.

106
00:09:11.025 --> 00:09:19.350
A molecule that binds it makes other drugs
bind ten times better, and tunes
degradation up or down depending on the
target.

107
00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:25.600
It is the first cooperative allosteric
ligand ever found for an E three ligase.

108
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:30.000
And it gives drug designers a new way to
tune selectivity.

109
00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:36.550
For example, reducing S A L L four
degradation could make these drugs safer.

110
00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:44.475
David: One sentence to remember: cereblon
has a hidden pocket, and it works like a
volume knob for degradation.

111
00:09:45.675 --> 00:09:50.975
You have been listening to Protein
Degradation English Corner, from Litshare.

112
00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:52.625
See you next time.
